The script `run_tests.py` in this folder is used to perform regression tests
using in-place example scripts and provided log files as reference.

What this single script does is to launch the selected LAMMPS binary
using a testing configuration defined in a `.yaml` file (e.g., `config.yaml`)
for the set of input scripts inside the given `examples/` subfolders,
and compare the output thermo with that in the existing log file with the same number of procs.
If there are multiple log files with the same input script (e.g., `log.melt.*.g++.1` and `log.melt.*.g++.4`),
the one with the highest number of procs is chosen.

The output includes the number of passed and failed tests and
an `output.xml` file in the JUnit XML format for downstream reporting.
The output and error of any crashed runs are logged.

A test with an input script is considered passed when the given LAMMPS binary produces
thermo output quantities consistent with those in the reference log file
within the specified tolerances in the test configuration `config.yaml` file.

With the current features, users can:

    + specify which LAMMPS binary version to test (e.g., the version from a commit, or those from `lammps-testing`)
    + specify the examples subfolders (thus the reference log files) seperately (e.g. from other LAMMPS versions or commits), or
    + specify a file that lists of the examples input scripts to test
    + specify tolerances for individual quantities for any input script to override the global values in the config file
    + launch tests with `mpirun` with all supported command line features (multiple procs, multiple paritions, and suffixes)
    + skip certain input files (whose names match specified patterns) if not interested, or packaged not installed, or no reference log file exists
    + set a timeout for every input script run if they may take too long
    + skip numerical checks if the goal is just to check if the runs do not fail

Some benefits include:

    + separating regression testing from building LAMMPS
    + performing quick and full regression tests
    + keeping track of the testing progress to resume the testing from the last checkpoint (skipping completed runs)
    + distributing the input list across multiple processes by
      splitting the list of input scripts into separate runs (there are ~800 input scripts under the top-level examples)
      taking the cost of the individual runs into account (see "Distributing the tests over multiple workers" below)

Input arguments:

    + the path to a LAMMPS binary (can be relative to the working directory)
    + a test configuration file (see tools/regression-tests/config.yaml for an example)
    + a text file that lists of folders where the input scripts reside and how many of them line by line, or
      a text file that list of input scripts, or
      the path to the top-level examples

Output:

    + failure.yaml : a dictionary of the failed runs and reasons (a subset of progress.yaml)
    + progress.yaml: full testing results of the tested input scripts with the status (completed, failed or skipped)
                     with error messages (for failed runs), the walltime reported by LAMMPS and the
                     measured wall-clock time of the run (both in seconds)
    + output.xml   :    testing results in the JUnit XML format
    + run.log      :       screen output and error of individual runs

Both YAML files are written incrementally (one line per tested input) and parse as a
single YAML mapping keyed by the input script name.

Each entry has two times: "walltime" is the total wall time as reported by LAMMPS, which
is only available for completed runs and has a resolution of one second, and "elapsed" is
the measured wall-clock time of the whole run, which is also recorded for runs that crash
or time out. The latter is the actual cost of a test and is what the JUnit XML file
records.

The JUnit XML file records one test case per input script with the elapsed time and one of
four outcomes: passed (all numerical checks passed), failure (the run completed but
numerical checks failed), error (the run crashed, timed out, or produced no log file),
or skipped (the input was not run, or it ran but nothing could be checked, e.g. when
the reference log file is missing). The test case classname is the example folder
relative to the examples top-level folder, so results from distributed runs can be
merged and compared across machines.

Where a run starts to deviate from the reference
------------------------------------------------

All rows of thermo output are compared against the reference log file, not only the
last one of each run, since where a deviation starts says a lot about what causes it:

    + a run that does not agree with the reference log file from its first thermo
      output cannot have diverged yet, so it is a difference in the model or in the
      setup until proven otherwise
    + a run that agrees at first and drifts apart towards the end is what chaotic
      amplification of rounding differences looks like: shortening the run or widening
      the tolerance is usually the right answer, and tightening the tolerance of the
      early steps keeps the test meaningful
    + a deviation that appears at one specific step is worth a closer look: a neighbor
      list rebuild, a fix that starts to act, or reading or writing a file

For every quantity that leaves its tolerance somewhere in the run, the run log and the
test report record the first deviating step, the largest deviation and the step where
it occurs, and the deviation at the end of the run. The progress file and the JUnit XML
file record how many quantities deviate ("diverged") and the earliest deviation as a
fraction of the run ("diverged_from"/"diverged-from"): a value near zero means that the
run differs from the start, a value near one that only its end diverges. The Markdown
summary of merge_results.py groups the failed runs by those two cases.

The verdict of a test is still decided by the last row of thermo output of each run, so
this does not by itself turn passing tests into failing ones. A test that passes but
deviates in between says so in its status.

Inputs that are not tested, and inputs that need to be fixed
------------------------------------------------------------

The example inputs were not written to be tests, so a number of them cannot be compared
against a reference log file at all, and some need to be corrected before their result
means anything. Those cases are recognized and reported rather than counted as failures:

    + input scripts that need a multi-partition run (neb, prd, tad, temper, fix pimd,
      universe style variables, ...) are skipped: they write one log file per partition
      and need their own test configuration with "-partition NxM" and a matching number
      of MPI processes
    + input scripts that use styles or commands not included in the tested binary can
      never run, so they are skipped without being run. The styles included in the
      binary are read from its -help output and compared with the styles that exist in
      the source tree (scanned from the style macros in the headers of all packages),
      so the reason names the style and the package it comes from. Only definite
      mismatches are screened out this way: a style name hidden behind a variable
      substitution or in an included file is not visible to this check. The optional
      --preflight flag closes that gap by validating every remaining input script with
      a "-skiprun" dry run first, which parses the whole script and instantiates every
      style with the actual binary while all run and minimize commands end after one
      step. A preflight failure only skips the input when the error says that a style
      or command is part of a package that is not enabled (or missing because of a
      dependency); any other failure is inconclusive and the input is tested normally.
      The preflight verdicts only depend on the binary configuration, so they can be
      cached in a file with --preflight-cache and are shared between workers and
      reused as long as the version and installed packages of the binary stay the same.
      In an --analyze run the statically screened out input scripts are left out of
      the generated input lists entirely and recorded with the reason for leaving
      them out in input-list-incompatible.txt
    + input scripts under examples/COUPLE are always left out: they couple LAMMPS to
      another code (or drive it from one), so they cannot be tested standalone, and
      several of them are not even LAMMPS input scripts
    + with --preflight-only the "-skiprun" run is the whole test: every input script
      runs with the configured MPI setup, but the loops of all run and minimize
      commands end after one step. This tests that the input script parses, that
      every style exists, that the setup (reading files, building neighbor lists,
      domain decomposition and initial communication) works, and that one step runs
      without crashing, at a small fraction of the cost of the full runs and without
      any numerical checks. Runs that complete are recorded with the "runtest"
      category (see REPORTING.md). Post-run analysis in some inputs (e.g. variables
      dividing by averages accumulated over the full run) cannot work with runs cut
      short: failures whose error message shows that are recorded as skipped rather
      than failed, and inputs that cannot work with -skiprun for other reasons are
      listed in the skip list of config_skiprun.yaml with the reasons documented
      there. The check-examples.yml workflow runs this over the whole examples tree.
    + input scripts without a reference log file cannot be checked against anything, so
      they are run with their run commands shortened to "smoke_steps" steps, which only
      tests that they do not crash. If the shortened run fails, which happens when a
      variable refers to a fix that only produces output after more steps, the input is
      run unchanged instead and flagged as needing manual attention.
    + input scripts whose initial velocities depend on the number of MPI processes
      (a "velocity create" command without "loop geom" on a system built with
      create_atoms) cannot match a reference log file that was written with a different
      number of processes; they are flagged for a fix in the repository. The number of
      MPI processes of a reference log file is read from the log file itself, since the
      number in its name counts the OpenMP threads as well
    + input scripts that need a good part of the timeout are production sized runs that
      should be shortened in the repository; they are flagged as well
    + input scripts that name a style which exists in no package at all, which is a typo
      or an outdated name rather than a package that is not installed
    + reference log files that belong to no input script in their folder, which is
      usually a typo in one of the two names and silently leaves the input unchecked

The last two are reported in an "attention" field of the progress file and as an
attribute of the JUnit test case, independently of whether the test passed or failed.

REPORTING.md in this folder documents all of that information and how a dashboard can
turn it into a work list.

The JUnit XML files from multiple (distributed) runs can be merged and summarized
with the merge_results.py script in this folder (which only needs the Python
standard library):

    python3 merge_results.py --title "Full Regression Test" \
            --output-xml regression.xml --json run.json --summary summary.md \
            --cost-file costs.json output-*.xml

This produces a merged JUnit XML file, a JSON file with all results and metadata
for archiving, and a Markdown summary (use "--summary -" to print it to the screen)
suitable for $GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY. With --compare previous-run.json, the summary
also reports the changes relative to an earlier run: new failures, fixed tests,
new tests, and removed tests. With --cost-file, the measured run times are recorded
for the next test run as described in the next section.

The detailed report of each failed test is always kept in the merged JUnit XML file.
It is left out of the JSON file, which is meant to be archived, unless a maximum
length is requested with --max-details.

Distributing the tests over multiple workers
--------------------------------------------

The cost of the individual runs spans several orders of magnitude, so splitting the
list of input scripts into equally long chunks leaves most workers idle while a few
of them are still running the expensive inputs. Instead, run_tests.py --analyze
estimates the cost of each input script and hands the most expensive one to the
worker with the least work assigned so far ("longest processing time first", the same
heuristic that CTest uses for tests with a COST property). Each worker's list is
ordered by decreasing cost as well, so the long runs are started first.

The cost estimates come from three sources, in this order:

    + the measured run times of a previous test run, passed in with --cost-file
      (this is by far the most reliable source; the file is written by
      merge_results.py --cost-file after that run)
    + the loop times in the reference log files of the input script, scaled from the
      number of procs of the log file to the number of procs used for the test and
      capped at the timeout (only a hint: those log files were generated on many
      different machines over the years)
    + for input scripts with neither, a pessimistic guess derived from the other
      input scripts, since a long run that is scheduled last delays the whole test run

Since the cost of a test depends on how it is run, each test configuration (serial,
4 MPI tasks, ...) needs its own cost file. A typical sequence for a full test run is:

    python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=build/lmp --config-file=config_serial.yaml \
            --examples-top-level=examples --analyze --num-workers=32 --cost-file=serial-costs.json

    for n in $(seq 0 31)
    do
        python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=build/lmp --config-file=config_serial.yaml \
                --list-input=input-list-$n.txt --output-file=output-$n.xml \
                --progress-file=progress-$n.yaml --failure-file=failure-$n.yaml \
                --log-file=run-$n.log --walltime-ref=$(cat walltime-ref.txt) &
    done
    wait

    python3 merge_results.py --title "Full Regression Test / Serial" \
            --json serial.json --cost-file=serial-costs.json output-*.xml

The cost file is updated in place, so the same command line works for the first run,
where the file does not exist yet, and for all later ones. Tests that did not run keep
their previously measured time.

Passing --walltime-ref avoids that all workers benchmark the LAMMPS binary against
each other at the same time. The --analyze run measures the reference walltime once
and writes it to walltime-ref.txt for this purpose.

Input scripts in the same folder are run by different workers unless their folder is
listed in the "keep_together" section of the test configuration file. That section is
for folders where one input script reads a file written by another one, or where two
input scripts write the same output file.

Limitations:

    + input scripts use thermo style multi (e.g., examples/peptide) do not work with the expected thermo output format
    + input scripts that require partition runs (e.g. examples/neb) need a separate config file, e.g., args: "--partition 3x1"
    + testing with accelerator packages (GPU, INTEL, KOKKOS, OPENMP) need separate config files, e.g., args: "-sf omp -pk omp 4"

The following Python packages need to be installed into an activated environment:

    python3 -m venv testing-env
    source testing-env/bin/activate
    pip install numpy pyyaml

For all the supported arguments, run:

    python3 tools/regression-tests/run_tests.py -h

Example uses (aka, tests for this script):

    1) Simple use (using the provided tools/regression-tests/config.yaml and the examples/ folder at the top level)
           python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=build/lmp --config-file=tools/regression-tests/config.yaml

    2) Use a custom testing configuration
           python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=/path/to/lmp_binary --config-file=/path/to/config/file/config.yaml

    3) Specify a list of example folders
           python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=/path/to/lmp_binary --config-file=/path/to/config/file/config.yaml \
                --example-folders="/path/to/examples/melt;/path/to/examples/rigid"

       The example subfolders can also be loaded from a text file list_subfolders1.txt:
           python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=/path/to/lmp_binary --config-file=/path/to/config/file/config.yaml \
                --list-subfolders=list_subfolders1.txt --output-file=output1.txt --progress-file=progress1.yaml \
                --log-file=run1.log

    4) Specify a list of example input scripts (e.g. obtained from running tools/regression-tests/get-quick-list.py)
            python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=/path/to/lmp_binary --config-file=/path/to/config/file/config.yaml \
                --list-input=input_list.txt

    5) Test a LAMMPS binary with the whole top-level /examples folder in a LAMMPS source tree
           python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=/path/to/lmp_binary --examples-top-level=/path/to/lammps/examples
                --config-file=tools/regression-tests/config.yaml

    6) Analyze the LAMMPS binary and whole top-level /examples folder in a LAMMPS source tree
       and generate separate input lists for 8 workers:
           python3 run_tests.py --lmp-bin=/path/to/lmp_binary --examples-top-level=/path/to/lammps/examples \
                --analyze --num-workers=8 --cost-file=costs.json

       The output of this run is 8 files folder-list-[0-7].txt that lists the subfolders
       and 8 files input-list-[0-7].txt that lists the input scripts under the top-level example folders.
       With these lists, one can launch multiple instances of run_tests.py simultaneously
       each with a list of example subfolders (Case 3), or with a list of input scripts (Case 4).
       Both are split such that all workers are expected to take the same time; the
       optional --cost-file is the measured run times from a previous test run.

An example of the test configuration `config.yaml` is given as below.

  ---
    lmp_binary: ""
    nprocs: ""
    args: "-cite none"
    mpiexec: "mpirun"
    mpiexec_numproc_flag: "-np"
    tolerance:
      PotEng:
        abs: 1e-4
        rel: 1e-7
      TotEng:
        abs: 1e-4
        rel: 1e-7
      Press:
        abs: 1e-4
        rel: 1e-7
      Temp:
        abs: 1e-4
        rel: 1e-7
      E_vdwl:
        abs: 1e-3
        rel: 1e-7
    overrides:
      in.rigid.tnr:
        Temp:
          abs: 1e-3
          rel: 1e-5
        Press:
          abs: 1e-2
          rel: 1e-4
    skip:
      [
        in.displ,
        in.displ2,
        in.*_imd*,
      ]
    keep_together:
      [
        PACKAGES/rhok,
      ]

    nugget: 1.0
    epsilon: 1e-16
    timeout: 180

Note that if nprocs is left empty "", as in the above example, the test for a given input script will use the maximum number of procs among the log files.
For instance, for the input script examples/melt/in.melt, there are 2 log files with 1 and 4 procs. The test for in.melt will then run with 4 procs and the output is compare against the 4-proc log file.  This is the typical configuration.

If nprocs is specified explicitly, for example, nprocs: "2", this value of nprocs will be used for ALL the input scripts (except for the valgrind test).
In this case, the reference log file is again the one with the maximum number of procs, that is, 4. One example of this configuration is for KOKKOS tests where
the runs are often `mpirun -np 2 lmp -in in.melt -k on g 2` (with the CUDA backend) or  `mpirun -np 2 lmp -in in.melt -k on t 2` (with the OpenMP backend).

An example of the list of example subfolders in a text file `list_subfolders1.txt`

    /home/codes/lammps/examples/melt 1
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/body 5
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/PACKAGES/dielectric 2
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/PACKAGES/tally 3

where the numbers are the number of input scripts (in.*) in the folders.


An example of the list of input scripts in a text file `input_list.txt`

    /home/codes/lammps/examples/melt/in.melt
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/body/in.body
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/body/in.cubes
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/PACKAGES/dielectric/in.confined
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/PACKAGES/tally/in.pe
    /home/codes/lammps/examples/PACKAGES/tally/in.force


Creating compatible log files
-----------------------------

The script `create_example_logs.sh` can be used to create or
recreate compatible example logs from all files in the current
folder that start with `in.` or from a list given on the
command line. The first argument is the LAMMPS executable
which is being launced using 1 and 4 MPI tasks.
